// Chillstation 13, second draft: a HUB station. The Commons sits in the
// middle with a corridor ring around it and a department on every side,
// which is the shape a station wants and the shape the first draft did
// not have -- that one put the crew in the top-left corner and grew
// south-east until it ran out of paper.
//
// Read the picture as five bands, north to south:
//
//        solar array (in space, north of the hull)
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//   |  Workshop   |     Bunks      |    Cargo Bay     |  y 4..9
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//   |             T H E   C O N C O U R S E           |  y 11
//   +------+------------------------------+-----------+
//   |E| W  |                              |   E   |E|
//   |V| e  |        T H E  C O M M O N S  |   a   |V|    y 13..18
//   |A| st |          (the only tiling)   |   st  |A|
//   +------+------------------------------+-----------+
//   |             T H E   C O N C O U R S E           |  y 20
//   +----------+--------------------------+-----------+
//   | Engineer |                          |   Cargo   |
//   +----------+     A T M O S P H E R I  |   Hall    |  y 22..34
//   |   Tank Annex   |        C S         |           |
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//
// EVERY ROOM IS PLATING BUT ONE. The Commons is the only finished deck
// on the station (glyphs '.', 'A', 'L', 'B', 'O' below); everywhere else
// the plates are off and the subfloor is the picture -- pipe, cable,
// paint and layer, read off the deck rather than out of a wiki. That is
// what Atmospherics has always looked like here and the reason it looked
// better than the rest of the station: a corridor that shows its
// plumbing is a corridor you can learn a station from. So the tiling is
// now a PLACE rather than a default, and it marks the one room the crew
// is meant to sit down in.
//
// EVERYTHING IS FED. Ten photovoltaic panels north of the hull and a
// generator and an SMES bank in Engineering charge one layer-1 feeder
// that reaches all nine APCs; each APC opens a layer-2 branch that runs
// under every light, every fan and every socket it owns. The first draft
// shipped two boot warnings on purpose (an unwired room was the
// construction playground); this one ships NONE, and the playground is
// now the empty blocked-out rooms and the space around the hull.
//
// ROOM TO GROW. Three tiles of vacuum on every side of the hull, a
// corridor ring that touches all four hull faces, and two rooms (Cargo
// Bay, Cargo Hall) built, lit, wired and deliberately empty. A new
// department bolts onto the ring by knocking out one wall run and
// wiring one APC -- no re-plumbing, no re-routing, no moving the crew.
//
// THE WAY OUT is a chamber, never a hole: two cycle-linked airlocks with
// three tiles of bare plating between them, at each end of the Commons
// band. Shape copied from tgstation's MetaStation "Escape Pod One"
// (_maps/map_files/MetaStation/MetaStation.dmm, (38,147)-(38,149)): an
// /obj/machinery/door/airlock/external at each end, three
// /turf/open/floor/plating between them, hull all the way round. The
// pair is CYCLE-LINKED (`cycle_links` below, tg's
// /obj/effect/mapping_helpers/airlock/cyclelink_helper): opening either
// one shuts the other, so the chamber cannot be held open at both ends.
// That is machinery rather than crew discipline -- the station is not
// vented by one impatient crewman.
//
// WINDOWS, twenty-three of them, and every one is a grille let into a
// HOLE in the wall rather than a picture hung on it (the tile under a
// pane is '_', never '#'). Nine look north out of the rank at the solar
// array, three west out of Engineering, three east out of the Cargo
// Hall, four into the two vestibules, and four are the tank annex's
// own -- fifteen in the hull and eight inside it. They ship
// glazed, because tg maps its window galleries assembled -- an
// /obj/effect/spawner/structure/window/reinforced, not bare rods for the
// crew to fit. A glazed pane is a BARRIER: it holds a room's air exactly
// as a shut door does (tg window.dm:405 `can_atmos_pass`). Prise one out
// of the hull and that room is open to space, which is the point of
// putting one there.
//
// Atmospherics, its gas storage and the tank annex were MOVED, not
// rebuilt: the whole block came south-west out of the first draft
// unchanged, so every layer, adapter, valve and net inside it is the one
// that was commissioned there. What changed is what it hangs off -- the
// supply main now climbs into a corridor instead of a back room, and the
// scrubbers main comes home from the Commons instead of from the only
// other room on the station.
//
// Demo: docs/chillstation-v2.png.
(
    name: "Chillstation 13",
    legend: {
        ' ': (turf: Space),
        '#': (turf: Wall),
        // Bare deck, and the station's DEFAULT. Everything under it is
        // visible: pipe, cable, and the paint that tells two mains apart.
        '_': (turf: Plating),
        // Finished tiling, and the Commons' alone (see the header).
        '.': (turf: Floor),
        // An internal airlock, standing on bare plating like every
        // threshold on the station.
        '+': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Door)),
        // An EXTERNAL airlock -- the leaves of the two EVA vestibules.
        // Same door '+' spawns; a glyph is a word in a picture, and a
        // door to vacuum should not read the same as a door to a
        // corridor.
        'E': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Door)),
        // The station's fixtures, on plating. Lowercase is the deck the
        // station is actually made of; the uppercase pair below is the
        // Commons' finished floor and nothing else.
        'a': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Apc)),
        // Standard ceiling tubes, in tg's default fluorescent white
        // (LIGHT_COLOR_DEFAULT "#f3fffa", code/__DEFINES/colors.dm:254 --
        // the colour /obj/machinery/light ships with, light.dm:24-28).
        'l': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Light), light: Some("#F3FFFA")),
        'o': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Outlet)),
        'c': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Canister)),
        // The power plant: a superconducting bank and a backup set, both
        // in Engineering, both standing on the feeder they charge.
        'm': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Smes)),
        'g': (turf: Plating, fixture: Some(Generator)),
        // Photovoltaics, standing in vacuum north of the hull where the
        // sun is. Quiet, modest watts, no moving parts.
        's': (turf: Space, fixture: Some(Solar)),
        // ---- the Commons, and only the Commons -------------------
        'A': (turf: Floor, fixture: Some(Apc)),
        'L': (turf: Floor, fixture: Some(Light), light: Some("#F3FFFA")),
        // The one warm fitting on the station: a tungsten bulb
        // (LIGHT_COLOR_TUNGSTEN "#FFD6AA", colors.dm:312) somebody put in
        // over the middle of the room and nobody has replaced. Four tiles
        // from the tube at (16, 13), so the deck between them is a
        // gradient from cool to warm and back -- the whole point of
        // coloured light, standing on one floor.
        'B': (turf: Floor, fixture: Some(Light), light: Some("#FFD6AA")),
        'O': (turf: Floor, fixture: Some(Outlet)),
    },
    rows: [
        "                                        ",
        "      s s s s s s s s s s               ",
        "                                        ",
        "   ####___########___########___#####   ",
        "   #__l____l_#__l____ol__#__l____l__#   ",
        "   #a________#a__________#a_________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #__l____l_#__l_____l__#__l____l__#   ",
        "   #####+##########+##########+######   ",
        "   #__l___l___l___a__l___l___l___l__#   ",
        "   #####_##########+###########_#####   ",
        "   ###_l____l_#.L...B..L.#_l____l_###   ",
        "   #__________#A........O#__________#   ",
        "   E_E________+..........+________E_E   ",
        "   #__________#..........#__________#   ",
        "   ###__cccc__#..........#________###   ",
        "   ###_l____l_#.L......L.#_l____l_###   ",
        "   #####_##########+###########_#####   ",
        "   #_l____l____l__l_____l___l___l___#   ",
        "   #####+##########+###########+#####   ",
        "   #a_m___g__#_c_ca_l___l__#a_______#   ",
        "   __________#_____________#l______l#   ",
        "   __________#_____________#_________   ",
        "   __l_____l_#_____c_______#_________   ",
        "   ###_###_###_________c___#_________   ",
        "   #al_____l_#_____________#l_______#   ",
        "   #_______________________#_______l#   ",
        "   #____________________o__##########   ",
        "   #_________#######+#######            ",
        "   #_________+_____________#            ",
        "   #_________###+###+###+###            ",
        "   #_________##___#___#___##            ",
        "   ############___#___#___##            ",
        "             ###############            ",
        "                                        ",
        "                                        ",
        "                                        ",
    ],
    // In the middle of the Commons, which is the whole argument of this
    // draft: a crewman wakes up at the centre of the station with a
    // department in every direction, rather than in the corner of the
    // only finished room.
    spawns: [(18, 16), (19, 16), (20, 16), (21, 16)],
    items: [
        (17, 16, "toolbox"),
        // ---- the Commons -----------------------------------------
        // A shift is long, and this is the room everybody walks
        // through. There used to be two loose dice on the floor here --
        // the only things on this map that did nothing useful, which
        // was the whole of what they were for. They are inside the Good
        // Clean Fun now, in the bag tg keeps them in, along with a deck
        // of cards and a toy that points at a wall. A die on the deck
        // is one die; a machine that hands out dice is a rec room.
        // ---- the chem bench (in the Bunks) ------------------------
        // Two beakers and a burner on the deck under the dispenser and
        // the reaction chamber. The beakers because chemistry is two
        // vessels and a pour, and the burner because the chamber is
        // bolted to a wall and a flame is not -- it is how you heat the
        // thing you are carrying, and it arrives with fifteen units of
        // spirit in it (items/burner.luau).
        (19, 5, "beaker"),
        (19, 5, "beaker"),
        (20, 5, "burner"),
        // The small glass, on the tile beside them. A dropper for
        // moving one unit at a time, two tubes to keep a batch in while
        // the beaker is needed for the next one, and a bottle to put
        // the finished thing away in. Every one of them shows its level
        // through its own glass, which is the whole reason a bench of
        // vessels reads at a glance instead of being clicked through.
        (21, 5, "dropper"),
        (21, 5, "bottle"),
        (22, 5, "test_tube"),
        (22, 5, "test_tube"),
        // ---- the Workshop ----------------------------------------
        // Everything the first draft scattered across an unfinished
        // room, now in the room whose job it is: sheet, tile, rod,
        // glass, the tools to fit them and the boards that turn a frame
        // into a machine. The rank's west room is where a shift learns
        // to build, and it is one door off the Concourse.
        (5, 6, "welder"),
        (6, 6, "crowbar"),
        (5, 7, "cable_coil"),
        (6, 7, "cable_coil"),
        (7, 6, "metal"),
        (7, 7, "metal"),
        (8, 6, "floor_tile"),
        (8, 7, "floor_tile"),
        (9, 6, "vendor_board"),
        (10, 6, "tool_vendor_board"),
        (11, 6, "games_vendor_board"),
        (9, 7, "tv_board"),
        (5, 8, "rod"),
        (6, 8, "glass"),
        (7, 8, "rglass"),
        (8, 8, "screwdriver"),
        // Two sheets of reinforced glass, stacked, as the gallery's
        // spare. The windows themselves ship glazed; what a shift
        // actually needs on hand is the glass to put one BACK.
        (9, 8, "rglass"),
        (9, 8, "rglass"),
        // Lighting stock: a shattered tube is something that happens, so
        // something to fix it with is something the station needs.
        (10, 7, "box_lights"),
        (10, 8, "light_replacer"),
        // ---- the atmospherics bench ------------------------------
        // The four things a technician actually carries. The RPD builds
        // and removes plumbing anywhere, the analyser reads a room or a
        // run, the painter is how a main gets its livery -- and paint
        // here is plumbing, so it is also how a main gets cut by mistake
        // -- and the spare meter is a gauge for wherever the next
        // question is.
        (15, 29, "rpd"),
        (16, 29, "analyzer"),
        (17, 29, "pipe_painter"),
        (18, 29, "pipe_meter"),
        (19, 29, "wrench"),
        // ---- the west EVA vestibule ------------------------------
        // The suit and the bottle, standing in the chamber they are
        // for. Everybody wakes up with a breath mask and three litres
        // of oxygen; this is the OTHER half of going outside -- seventy
        // litres to breathe and a garment that keeps the vacuum off the
        // skin, because a tank alone stops you suffocating and does
        // nothing about the pressure.
        //
        // The pairing is tg's: the engineer's EVA outfit issues the
        // space suit and the standard oxygen tank together
        // (code/modules/jobs/job_types/station_engineer.dm:75-82).
        // West rather than east because west is the side Engineering
        // and the Workshop are on, and the crew who go outside come
        // from those two rooms.
        (4, 15, "eva_suit"),
        (4, 15, "oxygen_tank"),
    ],
    structures: [
        // ---- the hull galleries ----------------------------------
        // Nine panes across the north rank, looking out at the solar
        // array; three west out of Engineering; three east out of the
        // Cargo Hall. Each is a hole in the hull with glass in it, so
        // each is a place the station can be opened by a crowbar rather
        // than by a bomb -- which is exactly why a station has them.
        (7, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (8, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (9, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (18, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (19, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (20, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (29, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (30, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (31, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (3, 23, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (3, 24, "grille", "reinforced"), (3, 25, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (36, 24, "grille", "reinforced"), (36, 25, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (36, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // And four into the two vestibules, so the halls can see who is
        // in the chamber and whether the outer door is open before it
        // matters. Same glass, same consequence if it goes: these are
        // all that stand between a corridor and a chamber that is
        // deliberately opened to space several times a shift.
        (5, 14, "grille", "reinforced"), (5, 16, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (34, 14, "grille", "reinforced"), (34, 16, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // ---- the air the crew breathes ---------------------------
        // Eight vents, one per room the crew stands in, each on the end
        // of a spur off the distribution main that rings the station.
        // They are ordinary machines placed like every other machine in
        // the plumbing -- a technician can unbolt one, carry it away and
        // wrench it down somewhere else, and the RPD will hand out more.
        //
        // The Cargo Bay is the one room with no fan, and that is the
        // expansion stub: distro reaches its wall and stops, so the
        // next department starts with one length of pipe and one vent
        // rather than with a survey.
        (11, 8, "vent"), (17, 8, "vent"), (7, 17, "vent"),
        (18, 17, "vent"), (22, 17, "vent"), (31, 14, "vent"),
        (6, 23, "vent"), (34, 23, "vent"),
        // ---- the Commons' card table ------------------------------
        // Two tables in the room's south-east corner, directly under
        // the Good Clean Fun and clear of both rows a shift walks the
        // Commons along. They are two rather than one because what a
        // station built out of separate squares does when you put two
        // down -- read as one surface -- ought to be visible in the
        // room a shift actually crosses (tg's `SMOOTH_GROUP_TABLES`,
        // tables_racks.dm:29-31). And because the machine that hands
        // out a deck of cards now has somewhere to deal onto: a card
        // put down on a table lands on the pixel it was pointed at,
        // which is what makes a hand of cards a hand rather than a
        // pile.
        (23, 17, "table"), (24, 17, "table"),
        // The scrubber that makes the department a LOOP rather than a
        // supply: it stands on the Commons' own branch circuit, on
        // layer 2, and takes the carbon dioxide the crew breathe out
        // back to Atmospherics.
        (16, 17, "air_scrubber", "scrubbing"),
        // THE BOTTLE RACK. Four sockets in the west hall, each beside
        // the through-main and mouthing south into it: the connector is
        // the component on its own tile, never raw pipe hidden under
        // one. Pull a port up with a wrench and that bottle stops
        // charging the ring.
        //
        // Four rather than the first draft's one, and the number is not
        // decoration -- it is the ring measured. That draft ran twenty
        // tiles of distribution between two rooms, and one bottle could
        // fill twenty tiles several times over. This one rings the
        // whole station: seventy-five tiles of layer-3 distro under the
        // Concourse and its spurs, which is very nearly four times as
        // much pipe to hold up. A reserve is sized against its loop or
        // it is sized against nothing, and the first hull breach is
        // where you find out which. This is the room where that
        // arithmetic is visible, standing on the deck in a row.
        (8, 17, "connector_port"),
        (9, 17, "connector_port"),
        (10, 17, "connector_port"),
        (11, 17, "connector_port"),
        // ---- where the department meets the station --------------
        // The supply main leaves Atmospherics through its own north
        // wall, crosses the south corridor in the open, and becomes the
        // station's distribution run. Three fittings, in a row, on deck
        // a technician can stand on:
        //   (20, 20) the isolation valve on the main, which is where
        //            tg puts one and what the wiki tells a technician
        //            to reach for first -- a valve wherever you might
        //            need to part a line without stopping the station.
        //            A per-layer preset, because a valve takes its
        //            layer from its entry and a main is not on the
        //            default one. It ships OPEN: a commissioned
        //            station's valves are, and an isolation point is a
        //            thing you CAN shut, not a thing that starts shut.
        //   (22, 20) the layer adapter that puts the layer-4 main onto
        //            the layer-3 run the station's vents are plumbed
        //            to. An adapter welds every layer it touches into
        //            ONE net; take it out with a wrench and the ring
        //            parts from the department, conserving every mole
        //            (tfs/tests/adapters_test.luau).
        //   (13, 20) the scrubbers main's own wheel, on the other side
        //            of the corridor and on layer 2, so the waste side
        //            can be cut without touching supply.
        (20, 20, "manual_valve_l4", "open"),
        (22, 20, "layer_adapter"),
        (13, 20, "manual_valve_l2", "open"),

        // ---- Atmospherics ----------------------------------------
        // Everything from here down came out of the first draft
        // unchanged, moved as one block. GAS STORAGE: two bottles on
        // connector ports feed the process line; two more hang off the
        // machines that need or make a second gas -- the mixer's other
        // intake, and the bottle the filter fills with what it pulls
        // out.
        (15, 22, "connector_port"),
        (17, 22, "connector_port"),
        (23, 26, "connector_port"),
        // THE PROCESS LINE, west to east. Everything the department does
        // to gas happens along one row of deck: separate, blend, push.
        // The filter's side mouth drops south into the bottle it fills;
        // the mixer's takes from the one beside it. Commissioned, not
        // shipped in a box: the map places each of these in a STATE, and
        // for a machine that has positions the state IS the switch. tg
        // maps `/on` subtypes for exactly this reason -- a station whose
        // air only starts moving once somebody walks to Atmospherics is
        // not a station.
        // A filter ROW, tg's shape: one machine per gas, in series down
        // the waste line, each dropping what it catches out of its side
        // mouth. Oxygen first, then carbon dioxide; what survives both
        // is the nitrogen the mixer blends back.
        (19, 23, "gas_filter_n2", "running"),
        (21, 23, "gas_filter_co2", "running"),
        (23, 23, "gas_mixer_airmix", "running"),
        (25, 23, "gas_pump", "running"),
        // Two gauges, either side of the machines that change the gas --
        // which is the only place a gauge earns its bolt.
        (20, 23, "meter"),
        (24, 23, "meter"),
        // THE LAYER ADAPTERS, and the reason the department can exist at
        // all. The station's vents are on piping layer 3
        // (structures/vent.luau, the distribution layer), while tg's
        // mains are layer 4 for supply and layer 2 for scrubbers. So:
        //   (26, 24) puts the pump's layer-3 outlet onto the layer-4
        //            supply main;
        //   (17, 27) brings the layer-2 scrubbers main home into the
        //            layer-3 process line, so scrubbed air is
        //            re-filtered rather than dumped;
        //   (25, 27) is the storage loop's own -- it puts the layer-2
        //            chamber draw onto the layer-3 line into the mixer's
        //            side intake.
        // (The fourth, at (22, 20) above, is the one that leaves the
        // department; it is listed with the station's fittings because
        // that is whose side of the bulkhead it is on.)
        (26, 24, "layer_adapter"),
        (17, 27, "layer_adapter"),
        (25, 27, "layer_adapter"),
        // The COLOUR adapter, joining the cyan process line to the green
        // chamber feed. Paint is plumbing here -- two runs whose colours
        // disagree are two networks -- so this is the one fitting that
        // lets a department keep its lines legible and still have them
        // be one body of gas.
        (14, 24, "color_adapter"),
        // ISOLATION, inside the department: tg puts a hand valve wherever
        // a technician needs to cut a leg out without stopping the
        // station, and this is the one on the green feed that charges the
        // oxygen and nitrogen chambers. The manual valve is the wheel you
        // turn in the dark; the digital one answers its panel and needs
        // the lights on. Both ship OPEN.
        (14, 28, "manual_valve", "open"),
        (16, 23, "digital_valve", "open"),
        // The waste-side pump, on the return riser: the scrubbers main
        // arrives here having crossed the station on its own and needs
        // pushing up into the process line.
        //
        // A VOLUMETRIC one, and that is the whole point of the choice. A
        // pressure pump works to a setpoint and stops -- put one here at
        // one atmosphere, against a process line already at one
        // atmosphere, and it has arrived before it started and the
        // return never moves. A return line does not want a pressure, it
        // wants a throughput, which is exactly what tg's volume pump is
        // for (volume_pump.dm: it "never asks").
        (18, 26, "volume_pump", "running"),
        // Bottled nitrogen off the first filter's side mouth. The
        // connector stands at (19,25) and mouths north into the catch
        // pipe at (19,24); that keeps the component off the raw run and
        // keeps its own tile's valid layer-4 crossing intact.
        (19, 25, "connector_port"),
        // ---- the tank annex --------------------------------------
        // Seven stationary reservoirs, one per substance this shift's
        // atmospherics runs on, each filled by the PLACEMENT rather than
        // by a prototype of its own -- tg ships twenty /tank/<gas>
        // subtypes and this map ships none. 23 000 kPa is tg's
        // `starting_pressure_percent = 0.5` of the 46 000 kPa rating
        // (tank.dm:39, 43).
        //
        // Deliberately UNPLUMBED. A tank shares its contents with every
        // net its four mouths reach, so a shared main through this row
        // would settle all seven into one mixture within a pass and the
        // store would be seven tanks of the same thing. The gaps between
        // them and the whole of rows 29-30 and 32 are where an engineer
        // runs pipe to whichever one they want today.
        (4, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("o2", 23000.0)),
        (6, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("n2", 23000.0)),
        (8, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("co2", 23000.0)),
        (10, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("plasma", 23000.0)),
        // The fifth is the WATER reservoir, and it is the odd one in the
        // row because water is the one substance here with a liquid
        // phase: there is no `water_vapor` to bottle, only water, which
        // at annex temperature belongs in the bottom of the tank rather
        // than hanging in it.
        //
        // A fill names a PRESSURE at T20C and the moles follow from
        // PV = nRT, so the arithmetic runs backwards from how full the
        // shell should be:
        //   half of 2 500 L of liquid = 1 250 L = 1 250 kg,
        //   1 250 000 g / 18.015 g/mol = 69 386 mol -- call it 69 000;
        //   69 000 x 8.314 x 293.15 / 2.5 m^3 = 67.27e6 Pa.
        // Hence 67 268 kPa, which is 69 000 mol, which is 1 243 L of
        // water once it has condensed: a tank a bit under half full,
        // with damp air over it.
        //
        // Plumb this one into a warm room and it RAINS -- the water
        // leaves as vapour, crosses the dew point on the far side, and
        // lands on the deck. That is the intended experiment, and it is
        // why this tank stands at the east end of the row, on the side
        // the corridor door is on.
        (12, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("water", 67268.0)),
        (5, 33, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("tritium", 23000.0)),
        (7, 33, "stationary_tank", "anchored", ("n2o", 23000.0)),
        // The temperature control unit, on the machine tile the annex
        // wired for it: L2 branch under its feet, the layer-3 bench run
        // stopping either side. Its one mouth auto-binds the east stub;
        // pipe from there to whichever tank is today's experiment and
        // the thermostat conditions what the tank feeds it. Ships
        // running at its T20C default like the rest of the commissioned
        // department -- a conditioner with nothing to condition idles.
        (10, 31, "thermomachine", "running"),
        // Two panes in the wall the annex shares with Engineering, so
        // the power room can read its own gas store off the deck, and
        // two in the wall it shares with the machine hall, so the tank
        // row can be read from the department. Glass stands in a HOLE in
        // the wall and never on it, which is why the four tiles under
        // these are plating -- and because the flood that names rooms
        // crosses a pane and not a door, Engineering, the annex and the
        // machine hall are ONE named region on the ledger. They are one
        // room; you can see across all of it.
        (6, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (10, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (13, 28, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (13, 29, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // Each chamber's pair, the way tg pairs them: an injector
        // pushing gas IN off the green feed, and a scrubber set to
        // SIPHON drawing it back out onto the yellow draw. Both ship
        // running -- a storage chamber nobody switched on is a sealed
        // room full of gas with no way to reach it.
        (15, 33, "air_injector", "injecting"),
        (15, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning"),
        (19, 33, "air_injector", "injecting"),
        (19, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning"),
        (23, 33, "air_injector", "injecting"),
        (23, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning"),
    ],
    // The two vestibule interlocks: each airlock names the direction its
    // partner lies in, and the link resolves only because BOTH of them
    // do (tg airlock.dm:225 `cyclelinkairlock()`, whose scan rejects a
    // door that does not point back). Opening either shuts the other.
    cycle_links: [
        (3, 15, East),
        (5, 15, West),
        (34, 15, East),
        (36, 15, West),
    ],
    // The Commons' two vending machines, standing finished at roundstart
    // against the east wall, and the socket both are plugged into --
    // unplug the Commons and the shopping stops, which is the point of
    // corded appliances.
    machines: [
        (24, 13, "vendor"),
        (24, 15, "tool_vendor"),
        // The third crate against the same wall, on the same outlet.
        // tg puts Good Clean Fun in the dormitory or the library; this
        // station has neither, and the Commons is the room a shift
        // actually crosses.
        (24, 16, "games_vendor"),
        // The chem bench, on the Bunks' north wall beside the room's
        // own socket at (21, 4) -- one and two tiles of cord, well inside
        // the six a corded appliance gets (docs/POWER.md). The socket
        // stands where the wall is: (18, 4) is under a hull window, and
        // a fitting mounts on masonry or it does not mount. A dispenser
        // and a reaction chamber standing together is the whole of what
        // a chemist needs: press, pour, heat, watch.
        //
        // In the Bunks rather than the Commons because the first draft
        // put it on the card tables, and a rec room whose tables are
        // occupied by glassware is not a rec room. The Bunks were the
        // one wired, lit, ventilated room with nothing in them that is
        // not reserved for growth (the Cargo Bay and Cargo Hall stay
        // empty on purpose) -- so the empty room becomes the lab, one
        // door off the Concourse.
        (19, 4, "chem_dispenser"),
        (20, 4, "chem_heater"),
        // The pipe dispenser, corded to the outlet beside it. Every
        // fitting the department is built out of comes off this shelf,
        // which is why it stands in the department rather than in the
        // Commons with the snacks.
        (25, 29, "pipe_dispenser"),
    ],
