        (12, 11, 4), (13, 11, 4), (14, 11, 4), (15, 11, 4), (16, 11, 4),
        (17, 11, 4), (18, 11, 4), (19, 11, 4), (20, 11, 4), (21, 11, 4),
        (22, 11, 4), (23, 11, 4), (24, 11, 4), (25, 11, 4), (26, 11, 4),
        (27, 11, 4), (28, 11, 4), (29, 11, 4), (30, 11, 4), (31, 11, 4),
        (24, 20, 4), (25, 20, 4), (26, 20, 4), (27, 20, 4), (28, 20, 4),
        (29, 20, 4), (30, 20, 4), (31, 20, 4), (32, 20, 4), (33, 20, 4),
        (34, 20, 4),
        // The spurs: up into the Workshop and the Bunks through the
        // rank's own wall, east into the hall, south into Engineering
        // and the Cargo Hall. Each ends one tile short of its fan, which
        // is where a unary machine wants its run.
        (11, 10, 4), (11, 9, 4), (17, 10, 4), (17, 9, 4),
        (31, 12, 4), (31, 13, 4), (34, 21, 4), (34, 22, 4),
        (6, 19, 4), (6, 20, 4), (6, 21, 4), (6, 22, 4),
        // THE SCRUBBERS MAIN, layer 2, from the Commons' scrubber back
        // down to the department. It crosses the distribution run at
        // (12, 18) and shares that tile with it -- two mains, one deck,
        // two bodies of gas, which is the entire reason piping layers
        // exist. The wheel at (13, 20) sits in the gap between (12, 20)
        // and (14, 20): shut it and the waste side is two nets.
        (15, 17, 2), (14, 17, 2), (13, 17, 2), (12, 17, 2), (12, 18, 2),
        (12, 19, 2), (12, 20, 2), (14, 20, 2),

        // ==== ATMOSPHERICS ========================================
        // The PROCESS LINE, layer 3: gas storage in the west, through
        // the filter and the mixer, to the pump. This is the
        // department's own working line and it is left unpainted,
        // because it is not distro and not waste -- it is where the mix
        // is made.
        (14, 23, 4), (15, 23, 4), (17, 23, 4), (18, 23, 4), (20, 23, 4),
        (22, 23, 4), (24, 23, 4), (26, 23, 4),
        // The mixer's side feed: its second mouth drops south to the
        // bottle on the port at (23, 26). The connector replaces the last
        // raw tile, so the two lengths end at its northward stub.
        (23, 24, 4), (23, 25, 4),
        // THE RETURN RISER, and it is deliberately NOT a straight climb
        // up column 18. The scrubbers main arrives at the adapter
        // (17, 27), crosses the volume pump at (18, 26), and then steps
        // WEST into column 17 to reach the process line at (17, 23).
        //
        // The step is the whole point. A pipe joins its orthogonal
        // neighbours -- there are no directional fittings here -- so two
        // runs side by side in adjacent columns are one run. Climbing
        // column 18 put the riser alongside the filter's catch line in
        // column 19 for three rows, and every one of those three was a
        // bypass around the pump: inlet and outlet were the same net,
        // holding the same moles, and the pump was scenery.
        (18, 27, 4), (18, 25, 4), (17, 25, 4), (17, 24, 4),
        // The filter's catch: one raw tile, then the socket one tile
        // south, and no further. It cannot run down column 19 either --
        // (19, 25) sits beside the pump's outlet at (18, 25), which
        // welded the nitrogen the filter had just separated straight back
        // onto the line feeding it. A dead end is what a bottle wants
        // anyway.
        (19, 24, 4),
        // The CO2 chamber's own feed, in engineering orange, fed by the
        // filter that catches it and by nothing else. This is the line
        // that makes the loop a LOOP: what a scrubber takes out of a
        // corridor ends up in the tank labelled for it, rather than in a
        // bottle somebody has to carry.
        (21, 24, 4), (21, 25, 4), (21, 26, 4), (21, 27, 4), (21, 28, 4),
        (21, 29, 4), (21, 30, 4), (21, 31, 4), (22, 31, 4), (22, 32, 4),
        (22, 33, 4),
        // THE STORAGE LOOP, layer 3 and separated from the process line
        // by COLOUR rather than by layer -- which is what tg does inside
        // a department, and what the paint rules were built for. FEED
        // (green) carries what the filter row pulls out of the waste down
        // to each chamber's injector; DRAW (yellow) brings it back up
        // from each chamber's siphoning scrubber to the mixer. The two
        // run parallel up the department and never join.
        (14, 32, 4), (15, 32, 4), (16, 32, 4), (17, 32, 4), (18, 32, 4),
        (14, 33, 4), (18, 33, 4), (14, 31, 4), (14, 30, 4), (14, 29, 4),
        (14, 27, 4), (14, 26, 4), (14, 25, 4),
        // DRAW is layer 2, because a scrubber is: every one of them on
        // the station binds on tg's scrubbers layer, and a chamber's
        // siphon is no exception. So the storage draw and the station's
        // waste main are two layer-2 nets that never touch, told apart
        // on sight by their paint.
        (15, 35, 2), (16, 35, 2), (17, 35, 2), (18, 35, 2), (19, 35, 2),
        (20, 35, 2), (21, 35, 2), (22, 35, 2), (23, 35, 2), (24, 35, 2),
        (25, 35, 2), (26, 35, 2), (26, 27, 2), (26, 28, 2), (26, 29, 2),
        (26, 30, 2), (26, 31, 2), (26, 32, 2), (26, 33, 2), (26, 34, 2),
        // ...and the short layer-3 link that carries it into the mixer's
        // side intake, through the adapter at (25, 27).
        (24, 27, 4), (23, 27, 4),
        // The SUPPLY main, layer 4 and blue: tg's own convention,
        // counted across every map file it ships (`vent_pump/on/layer4`,
        // 2091 instances against one on any other layer). It leaves the
        // pump, runs west under the department and climbs to the
        // bulkhead at (18, 21).
        (18, 25, 8), (19, 25, 8), (20, 25, 8), (21, 25, 8), (22, 25, 8),
        (23, 25, 8), (24, 25, 8), (25, 25, 8), (26, 25, 8), (18, 24, 8),
        (18, 23, 8), (18, 22, 8), (18, 21, 8),
        // The SCRUBBERS main, layer 2 and red (tg's
        // `vent_scrubber/on/layer2`, 1860 against one). It arrives from
        // the Commons through (14, 21) and comes down the same shaft the
        // supply climbs -- which is the entire reason piping layers
        // exist: these two share tiles at (18, 23), (18, 25) and
        // (14, 23) and are separate bodies of gas on every one of them.
        (14, 21, 2), (14, 22, 2), (14, 23, 2), (14, 24, 2), (14, 25, 2),
        (14, 26, 2), (14, 27, 2), (15, 27, 2), (16, 27, 2),
        // The tank annex's bench run, layer 3, with a machine-sized gap
        // at (10, 31) for whatever wants standing there. It is
        // deliberately NOT joined to the department's green chamber feed
        // one tile east: a debug bench wired into the live station is a
        // debug bench that breaks the station. One length laid on the
        // door tile at (13, 31) joins them the day somebody wants that.
        // Unpainted for the same reason -- it belongs to no numbered
        // main yet.
        (12, 31, 4), (11, 31, 4), (9, 31, 4), (8, 31, 4),
